CHUNK({"chunk":"E","module":"m2","topics":[{"id":"m2-gm-basics","title":"Genetically modified crops: what and why","short":"GM crop basics","blurb":"What GM crops are, why agriculture needs them, the science of inserting a gene (Bt), and how GM differs from conventional breeding.","source":"Lecture W2 (GM crops), slides 2–5","tabs":[{"title":"What they are","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\"><b>GM crops</b>: <b>DNA altered by genetic engineering</b> to add traits not natural to the species.</p>\n          <ul>\n          <li>Aims: <b>yield</b>, pest/disease resistance, tolerance of <b>drought, salinity, extreme temperatures</b>.</li>\n          <li>Common traits: <b>pest resistance, herbicide tolerance</b>, drought or disease resistance.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note example\"><b>Bt crops</b>: gene from soil bacterium <b><em>Bacillus thuringiensis</em></b>; kills specific pests, safe for humans. <b>Cotton and corn</b>.</div>\n        "},{"title":"GM vs conventional","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n          <thead><tr><th></th><th>Conventional</th><th>GM</th></tr></thead>\n          <tbody>\n          <tr><td>Method</td><td>Crossing, <b>many generations</b></td><td><b>Direct gene insertion</b></td></tr>\n          <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slow, less predictable</td><td><b>Faster</b>, targeted</td></tr>\n          <tr><td>Gene source</td><td>Same or related species</td><td><b>Unrelated species</b> too</td></tr>\n          </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is a GM crop?","back":"A plant whose <b>DNA is altered by genetic engineering</b> to add traits it does not have naturally."},{"front":"Source of the Bt gene?","back":"The soil bacterium <b><em>Bacillus thuringiensis</em></b>."},{"front":"Key advantage of GM over conventional breeding?","back":"Faster, precise, and can use genes from <b>unrelated species</b>."}],"cardCount":5,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-gm-india","title":"GM crops in India and Bt cotton","short":"GM in India, Bt cotton","blurb":"How India regulates GM crops (MoEFCC, GEAC), which crops are approved or on trial, how Bt crops work, and Bt cotton's impact.","source":"Lecture W2 (GM crops), slides 6–10","tabs":[{"title":"Regulation and status","html":"\n          <p class=\"lead\">Regulator: <b>MoEFCC</b>; apex body: <b>GEAC</b> (Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee).</p>\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n          <thead><tr><th>Crop</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>\n          <tbody>\n          <tr><td><b>Bt cotton</b></td><td><b>2002</b>; <b>only GM crop approved</b></td></tr>\n          <tr><td><b>Bt brinjal</b></td><td>Cleared <b>2009</b>, then <b>moratorium</b></td></tr>\n          <tr><td><b>GM mustard</b></td><td>Under review</td></tr>\n          <tr><td>Rice, chickpea, maize</td><td>Research/testing</td></tr>\n          </tbody>\n          </table>\n        "},{"title":"Bt cotton impact","html":"\n          <ul>\n          <li>Bt toxin targets <b>bollworms</b>, <b>corn borers</b>.</li>\n          <li><b>Over 90%</b> of India's cotton area.</li>\n          <li>Bollworm insecticide down <b>up to 50–60%</b>.</li>\n          <li>India = world's <b>largest cotton producer</b>.</li>\n          <li>Problems: <b>secondary pests</b> (whiteflies, aphids), bollworm resistance.</li>\n          <li>Fix: <b>refuge planting</b>, integrated pest management.</li>\n          </ul>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Apex body that evaluates GM crop safety in India?","back":"<b>GEAC</b>, Genetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (under MoEFCC)."},{"front":"Only GM crop approved for cultivation in India, and its year?","back":"<b>Bt cotton, 2002</b>."},{"front":"What happened to Bt brinjal?","back":"Cleared in <b>2009</b>, then put under a <b>moratorium</b>."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-bt-maharashtra","title":"Case study: Bt cotton in Maharashtra","short":"Bt cotton case study","blurb":"How Bt cotton spread in Maharashtra, what it did to farmer income, the problems that followed (Vidarbha), and the lessons.","source":"Lecture W2 (GM crops), slides 11–15","tabs":[{"title":"Adoption and income","html":"\n          <ul>\n          <li><b>Maharashtra</b>: earliest large-scale adopter after <b>2002</b>; <b>over 90%</b> by <b>2010</b>.</li>\n          <li>Spread by <b>farmer-to-farmer communication</b>; seeds via <b>private seed companies</b>.</li>\n          <li><b>25–50%</b> pesticide savings early; shift to <b>market-oriented</b> farming.</li>\n          </ul>\n        "},{"title":"Problems and lessons","html":"\n          <ul>\n          <li>Bollworm resistance, secondary pests, costly <b>hybrid Bt seeds</b>, rising debt.</li>\n          <li>Droughts in rainfed <b>Vidarbha</b>.</li>\n          <li>Lesson: <b>GM alone is not enough</b>; needs irrigation, pest management, <b>crop insurance, fair seed pricing</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Share of Maharashtra cotton area under Bt by 2010?","back":"<b>Over 90%</b>."},{"front":"Pesticide cost savings reported by Maharashtra farmers?","back":"<b>25–50%</b> in the first few years."},{"front":"Rainfed Maharashtra region where Bt cotton struggled?","back":"<b>Vidarbha</b>."}],"cardCount":5,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-gm-flowers","title":"GM flowers and floriculture","short":"GM flowers","blurb":"GM ornamental plants: blue roses and carnations, the countries selling them, India's potential, and why florists profit.","source":"Lecture W2 (GM crops), slides 17–20","tabs":[{"title":"GM ornamentals","html":"\n          <ul>\n          <li>Traits: <b>colour, fragrance, shelf life</b>, wilting resistance.</li>\n          <li><b>Blue rose</b>; <b>GM carnations</b> (vase life, new colours).</li>\n          <li>Sold by <b>Japan, Australia, Colombia</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n          <div class=\"note heads-up\">Slides differ: <b>blue rose</b> first commercial GM ornamental vs <b>Japan's blue carnation</b> first. Know both.</div>\n        "},{"title":"India and florists","html":"\n          <ul>\n          <li>India researching GM <b>roses, marigolds, chrysanthemums</b>; rules <b>more flexible</b> than for food crops.</li>\n          <li>Florists gain: <b>longer shelf life</b>, premium colours, <b>year-round supply</b>.</li>\n          </ul>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Countries that commercialised GM flowers?","back":"<b>Japan, Australia, Colombia</b>."},{"front":"Two famous GM ornamentals?","back":"<b>Blue rose</b> and GM (blue) <b>carnations</b>."},{"front":"Flowers India is researching for GM?","back":"<b>Roses, marigolds, chrysanthemums</b>."}],"cardCount":4,"formulaCount":0},{"id":"m2-gm-debate","title":"GM crops: benefits, risks and the future","short":"GM pros, cons, future","blurb":"Economic and environmental benefits, ecological and health risks, socio-economic and ethical issues, public perception, the global picture and what comes next.","source":"Lecture W2 (GM crops), slides 16, 21–30","tabs":[{"title":"Benefits and risks","html":"\n          <div class=\"vs\">\n          <div class=\"side\"><h4>Benefits</h4><ul>\n          <li>More yield, <b>same land</b>; <b>less pressure on forests</b>.</li>\n          <li>Less spraying: lower <b>greenhouse gas emissions</b>.</li>\n          <li>Longer shelf life, higher nutrition.</li>\n          </ul></div>\n          <div class=\"side\"><h4>Risks</h4><ul>\n          <li><b>Cross-pollination</b> with wild plants.</li>\n          <li><b>Resistant weeds</b> (herbicide overuse).</li>\n          <li><b>Patented seeds</b> bought each season; <b>corporate control</b>.</li>\n          <li>Loss of traditional crop diversity; poor <b>labelling</b>.</li>\n          </ul></div>\n          </div>\n        "},{"title":"Global and future","html":"\n          <table class=\"tbl\">\n          <tbody>\n          <tr><td>Wide adoption</td><td><b>USA, Brazil, Argentina</b></td></tr>\n          <tr><td>Strict</td><td><b>European Union</b></td></tr>\n          <tr><td>India</td><td><b>Crossroads</b>: cautious yet progressive</td></tr>\n          </tbody>\n          </table>\n          <ul>\n          <li>Future: <b>CRISPR</b> gene editing; <b>nitrogen-use efficiency</b>, drought tolerance; <b>GM mustard</b> next.</li>\n          </ul>\n        "}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How do GM crops reduce pressure on forests?","back":"Higher yields mean <b>less land expansion</b>."},{"front":"Why do herbicide-tolerant crops cause a problem?","back":"Overuse breeds <b>resistant weeds</b> needing stronger chemicals."},{"front":"Countries that widely grow GM crops?","back":"<b>USA, Brazil, Argentina</b>."}],"cardCount":6,"formulaCount":0}],"figures":[]});
